=== WHAT THE EXPORT FIX WAS (stage 2 unblock) ===
 The app exported the RETIRED my:skiko-gfx/renderer.render-frame, so the host
 never ticked it (input.rs::dispatch_frame drives UI guests through
 wasi:input-handlers/frame-handler ONLY). Now:
   world  wandr:video-player-app/video-player-app  (was my:skiko-gfx/skiko-ui;
          package.toml `world` updated to match)
   export wasi:input-handlers/frame-handler@0.0.2  (on-frame + on-resize)
   export wandr:ui-shell/frame-pacing@0.1.0
   import wasi:canvas/{types,draw,embedding}@0.0.2 + wandr:video/decoder@0.1.0
 wit/deps/ gained input-handlers-002, ui-shell, wasi-canvas (copied from
 wandr.powermenu, the reference reactor).

=== TWO NEW STAGE-2 FINDINGS (from making it actually show pixels) ===
 4. DECODE-TO-SURFACE IS NOT SELF-DRIVING — it rides the guest's canvas present.
    On desktop the host composites video surfaces inside
    wasi_canvas_002_impl.rs:1377 `context.present()`. The first cut imported NO
    canvas ("we draw nothing, the video is above-ui") and the window stayed
    blank white while the control path reported a perfect 300/300. A video guest
    MUST import wasi:canvas and present every frame; the player now clears to
    opaque black and presents, so every pixel of picture on screen came from the
    decoder. Same root shape as stage-1 gap #3 (surfaceless guests never
    composite). Worth stating explicitly in the contract docs.
 5. HOLDING the decoded-frame handle is the ONLY way a guest can delay a frame,
    and it is what makes playback real-time. Presenting everything drained per
    tick raced 10 s of video through in <1 s; the first cut "solved" that by
    DISCARDING future frames (and still counting them presented — a lying
    metric). The player now keeps one pending frame and presents it when the
    clock catches up, which also back-pressures decode for free (`presented`
    stops advancing -> the DECODE_AHEAD gate stops feeding). This is finding #1
    (present(at-ns) unusable) seen from the client side.

=== EARLIER FINDINGS (unchanged, still open) ===
 1. present(at-ns) IS NOT USABLE — a guest cannot name a host monotonic instant.
    Player passes at_ns=0 and self-paces. LEAD: on-frame's `nanos` is likely the
    shared timeline; make monotonic_now_ns use the SAME source, or add now().
 2. Decode-ahead is UNBOUNDED at the CONTRACT level (queue-full never triggers);
    the player bounds it guest-side. A guest could still decode a whole file into
    host memory.
 3. The SCHEDULED queue is drained ONLY by composite_video_surfaces, so
    surfaceless/--run-once guests never present. --run-once is a deliberate
    special case: DO NOT change it.

=== HOW TO RUN / VERIFY (both traps cost time — read before rerunning) ===
 HOST BINARY: runtime/wandr-host/target/x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu/release/wasm-android-host
   NOT target/release/ — a stale host-triple build lives there and fails at
   instantiate with "decoded-frame ... resource implementation is missing",
   which mimics the stale-zygote error. Check mtime first.
   ([[reference_host_build_scripts]] now records this.)
 BUILD+INSTALL:
   (cd apps/user/wandr.video.player && cargo build --target wasm32-wasip2 --release)
   cp apps/user/wandr.video.player/target/wasm32-wasip2/release/wandr_video_player.wasm \
      apps/user/wandr.video.player/components/ui.wasm     # cdylib = UNDERSCORES
   WANDR_APPS_ROOT=~/.local/share/wandr-apps $HOST --install apps/user/wandr.video.player
   WANDR_APPS_ROOT=~/.local/share/wandr-apps $HOST --app wandr.video.player
 VISUAL PROOF WITHOUT A HUMAN WATCHING: WSLg is ROOTLESS, so ffmpeg x11grab of
   the root captures BLACK. Run with WAYLAND_DISPLAY unset (forces winit X11),
   find the window by WM_CLASS ("wasm-android-host") in `xwininfo -root -tree`,
   then `xwd -id <wid>` -> ffmpeg -> png. Script kept at scratchpad/capture2.sh;
   worth promoting into repros/ if reused.

=== STAGE 3 DONE (2026-07-22, host commit 6a5178f) ===
 crates/wandr-video/src/backends/vaapi.rs — HW H.264 via cros-codecs + cros-libva,
 VA-allocated surfaces (no GBM), registered at priority 10 so HW is tried first.
 VERIFIED on popos i965: 300/300 AUs under require_hardware (so software could NOT
 have silently served it), every PTS tag back exactly once, frame 150 pulled back
 and EYEBALLED (sharp, correct colour, no U/V swap). tests/vaapi_hw.rs is that
 run; it SKIPS without a GPU. All 14 pre-existing wandr-video tests still pass.
 Fallback proven live: on WSL (no VA display) it declines and openh264 plays
 300/300 in real time.

 ‼️ NEW FINDING #6 — THE TWO BACKENDS DISAGREE ON TIMESTAMP SEMANTICS.
   The Decoder contract says I420Ref.timestamp_us is the chunk's timestamp
   "carried through the codec unchanged". vaapi does exactly that: cros-codecs
   carries the tag on the decoded handle, so frames come out in DISPLAY order each
   holding the tag of the AU that DECODED it. openh264 instead pairs the i-th
   input tag to the i-th output frame with a FIFO, i.e. it REORDERS tags to
   ascending — its own module header admits that is valid only without B-frames.
   Same input, two meanings; a player switching HW<->SW sees its timestamps change
   meaning underneath it.
   Root cause is upstream of both: an Annex-B ELEMENTARY STREAM HAS NO PTS. The
   player and the test synthesise `index * 1/30` from BITSTREAM order — a DTS.
   bbb.h264 is 247 B-frames deep (ffprobe: 1 I, 51 P, 247 B), so decode order !=
   display order and the synthesised tag is simply the wrong quantity.
   PROVEN, not assumed: output tag order is 0, 100000, 133333, 66666, … and the AU
   at bitstream index 1 (tag 33333) surfaces at display position 6 — exactly the
   I,P,B,B,B,B,B decode order -> I,B,B,B,B,B,P display order of a 5-B GOP.
   THE FIX IS A CONTAINER, NOT A CODEC: real PTS per AU from the demuxer (guest-
   side by design — proposals/wasi-media-source/NOTES.md). Feed true PTS and
   vaapi's output is naturally monotonic while openh264's FIFO becomes the broken
   one. Settle the contract wording in stage 4 and fix openh264 to match.

 NOT DONE ON PURPOSE (each is a decision, not a forgotten TODO):
   * H.264 ONLY. cros-codecs also does VP8/VP9/HEVC/AV1 and adding them is
     mechanical, but BOTH boxes are Intel Gen7 with no decode block for any of
     them — an unrunnable HW lane that claims a codec is the exact trap the
     fallback contract exists for. Add each when there is hardware to prove it.
   * NO ENCODE. Both boxes advertise H264 VAEntrypointEncSlice so it is a real gap,
     not an impossible one — but the encoder lane feeds live calls (congestion
     control, forced keyframes, bitrate retune) and playback exercises none of it.
     openh264 keeps encoding until a test exists that would catch a HW regression.
   * NO SURFACE POOL. Only ever required by NVDEC, which is gone from the fleet.
     Hygiene now, not a blocker.
   * TIER 1 (zero-copy export_prime -> DMA-buf) is probed-AVAILABLE on both boxes
     but NOT consumed: the I420Ref contract forces a CPU readback regardless. That
     is the prize for the zero-copy present path and it belongs with finding #1
     (present(at-ns)), not here.

 HOW TO RUN THE HW TEST (needs a GPU; skips without one):
   WANDR_TEST_H264=<annex-b .h264> [WANDR_DUMP_DIR=dump] \
     cargo test --features vaapi --test vaapi_hw -- --nocapture
   On popos the synced crate is at ~/wandr-video-hwtest (crates/wandr-video +
   crates/wandr-vpx-sys + vendor/cros-codecs; the [patch.crates-io] path is
   ../../vendor/cros-codecs, so that layout matters). Build NATIVELY there —
   popos glibc 2.39 < WSL 2.41, so a WSL-built binary will not run.

 GOTCHAS BAKED IN:
   * vendor/cros-codecs = crates.io 0.0.6 + TWO patches, both marked `wandr patch`
     in-source and documented in vendor/cros-codecs/WANDR-PATCHES.md. On an
     upgrade, losing patch 1 is a SILENT regression and losing patch 2 is a build
     error. The load-bearing one widens VaapiDecodedHandle::surface() to pub —
     with a custom VA-allocated VideoFrame there is NO other route to the pixels
     (to_native_handle MOVES the Surface into the backend and Surface is not
     cloneable; upstream's own frame types dodge this by being GBM-backed, which
     is exactly what fails on every machine here).
   * wandr-host has NO [workspace] — crates/wandr-video is standalone with its own
     Cargo.lock, so [patch.crates-io] had to go in BOTH Cargo.tomls; with only the
     host's, a standalone `cargo build` silently uses the UNPATCHED crates.io copy.
   * The `vaapi` feature is OFF by default — the only codec feature linking a
     SYSTEM library (libva). build-host-linux.sh PROBES pkg-config and enables it
     only when libva is present (VAAPI=0/1 to force). CI installs libva-dev and
     builds the Linux leg with p3-async,vaapi so the shipped config is the
     compiled one.

